#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Streams Kotak Neo market quotes over websockets into the Redis hash `kotak:quotes:live` until stopped.

    bin/kotak/instruments/websocket_quotes                                        # instruments from the Redis set kotak:quotes:subscriptions
    bin/kotak/instruments/websocket_quotes --tokens "nse_cm|11536,mcx_fo|565899"
    bin/kotak/instruments/websocket_quotes --per-socket 50

    redis-cli SADD kotak:quotes:subscriptions "nse_cm|11536" "nse_fo|42020"
    redis-cli HGET kotak:quotes:live "TCS-EQ"
    redis-cli HGETALL kotak:quotes:instruments

Self-contained: it logs in with `KotakAPI` and carries its own connection, authentication, subscription, acknowledgement and binary packet decoding for Kotak's HSM market feed at `wss://mlhsm.kotaksecurities.com`, the feed Kotak's own Python SDK (`neo_api_client`) streams from, so it needs no other feed process or key.

**Instruments** are `--tokens` when given, otherwise the members of the Redis set `kotak:quotes:subscriptions`, each as `EXCHANGE|TOKEN` with Kotak's lowercase segment names - `nse_cm`, `bse_cm`, `nse_fo`, `bse_fo`, `cde_fo`, `nse_com`, `bse_cd`, `bse_co` or `mcx_fo` - and the instrument's `pSymbol` from Kotak's scrip master. Each is named with its trading symbol (`pTrdSymbol`) from Kotak's public scrip master files for today, downloaded at startup; an instrument those do not name takes the trading symbol the feed's first snapshot carries. One websocket carries up to `--per-socket` instruments, 100 by default and at most, because every instrument takes two of the 200 subscriptions Kotak allows on one channel.

**Keys.**

- `kotak:quotes:live`, a hash of the latest tick per instrument as JSON, keyed by the instrument's trading symbol (`TCS-EQ`), or by its `EXCHANGE|TOKEN` while it has no name. A tick is replaced as the next one arrives. It is not cleared at startup, so an instrument no longer subscribed keeps its last tick.
- `kotak:quotes:instruments`, a hash of every subscribed `EXCHANGE|TOKEN` to its trading symbol, or to an empty string when unnamed. Replaced whole at startup, and filled in when a snapshot names an instrument.
- `kotak:quotes:stream`, a Redis Stream with every tick in arrival order as a `tick` field, which `bin/kotak/instruments/store_quotes_to_db` writes to `kotak.ticks`. Capped at about 1,000,000 entries, so ticks that arrive while the persister is down for long enough are trimmed before they are persisted.

**The tick.** Every instrument is subscribed twice: as a scrip topic (`sf|EXCHANGE|TOKEN`) for its prices and quantities, and as a depth topic (`dp|EXCHANGE|TOKEN`) for its five-level order book. A topic arrives once as a snapshot and then as updates carrying only the fields that changed, so the script keeps each topic's latest values and writes a whole tick for an instrument every time either of its topics changes. A tick is written only once the scrip snapshot has arrived.

| Tick field | Scrip topic field |
| --- | --- |
| `last_price` | 5 (`ltp`) |
| `last_quantity` | 6 (`ltq`) |
| `average_price` | 13 (`ap`) |
| `volume` | 4 (`v`) |
| `buy_quantity`, `sell_quantity` | 7 (`tbq`), 8 (`tsq`) |
| `ohlc` | 20 (`op`), 15 (`h`), 14 (`lo`), 21 (`c`, the previous session's close) |
| `change` | Computed as the percentage of `last_price` over `close` |
| `oi` | 22 (`oi`), which cash instruments do not send |
| `last_trade_time` | 3 (`ltt`), epoch seconds |
| `exchange_timestamp` | 2 (`fdtm`), epoch seconds |
| `depth` | The depth topic: prices 2 to 6 (buy) and 7 to 11 (sell), quantities 12 to 16 and 17 to 21, orders 22 to 26 and 27 to 31 |

`oi_day_high` and `oi_day_low` are always `None`. `mode` is `full` once the depth topic has arrived and `quote` before. Prices are integers divided by the topic's multiplier (scrip field 23, depth field 32) times ten to its precision (scrip field 24, depth field 33). On MCX every quantity, open interest and order book quantity included, is lots times Kotak's own lot size; on NSE and BSE quantities are units.

**The protocol.** Every request and response is a binary frame that begins with its length in two big-endian bytes and then a one-byte frame type. The connection request (type 1) carries the REST access token, the session id and the source `JS_API` as length-prefixed fields. Kotak answers with a type 1 frame whose status is `K` for accepted, and with the number of data frames after which it wants an acknowledgement. Subscriptions (type 4) list up to 100 `sf|` or `dp|` topic names on channel 1, and are acknowledged with a type 4 status. Data frames (type 6) begin with a four-byte message number when acknowledgements were asked for; after that many data frames the script sends an acknowledgement (type 3) carrying the last message number. Each data frame then holds packets that are either a snapshot (`S`, 83) - topic id, topic name, the numeric fields as four-byte signed integers in field order, and string fields by id - or an update (`U`, 85) - topic id and the numeric fields again, where -2147483648 means unchanged.

**Sessions.** Each connection reads the access token and session id in force when it connects, so a login made by any process is picked up. When Kotak refuses the connection, or a socket fails to open six times running, it logs in again through `KotakAPI` - one socket at a time - before reconnecting. A reconnect otherwise backs off from 1 to 60 seconds.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, 1 when a login fails or a socket still cannot open after logging in again, and 2 when there is nothing to subscribe to or `--per-socket` is out of range. Reads only - it places no orders.
"""

